                <pb facs="0184" n="112" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg112"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 774/665–666.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Bryan to the
                            Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Lisbon</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1909-10-12"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October 12, 1909</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 552.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the circular instructions,
                    file 774, of September 1, 1909, with regard to the International Opium
                    Commission, I have the honor to report that, prompted by the communication which
                    I made to the foreign office on this subject when transmitting the substance of
                    the said circular, the Portuguese Government has taken immediate action by
                    naming a commission to thoroughly consider the recommendation of our Government
                    on this subject. I inclose a copy, with translation, of the proclamation.
                    Following is a list of the commissioners: Messrs. Jose de Azevedo Castello
                    Branco, Arnaldo Guedes Rebello, Francisco Diogo de Sa, Jose Navarro de Andrade,
                    Oscar Potier, Annibal Augusto Sanches de Sousa Miranda.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Page
                    Bryan.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d126-a1.xml" xpointer="d126-a1"/>
